Refactor `summaries` into smaller methods: one to fetch orders, one to fetch items, and one to calculate totals. Each method should have a single responsibility and be easier to test and maintain. For example:

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<OrderSummary> summaries(LocalDate day) {
    List<Order> dayOrders = orders.findByCreatedDate(day);
    return dayOrders.stream()
            .map(order -> new OrderSummary(order.getId(), orderItemsService.countItems(order.getId()), orderItemsService.calculateTotal(order.getId())))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
}

@Service
public class OrderItemsService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderItemRepository items;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public int countItems(long orderId) {
        return items.countByOrderId(orderId);
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public BigDecimal calculateTotal(long orderId) {
        return items.findByOrderId(orderId).stream()
                .map(OrderItem::getPrice)
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
```

Use a JOIN query to fetch all items for the orders in a single query. For example:

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<OrderSummary> summaries(LocalDate day) {
    List<Order> dayOrders = orders.findByCreatedDate(day);
    List<OrderItem> allItems = items.findByOrderIds(dayOrders.stream().map(Order::getId).collect(Collectors.toList()));
    Map<Long, List<OrderItem>> orderItemsMap = allItems.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(OrderItem::getOrderId));
    List<OrderSummary> result = new ArrayList<>();
    for (Order order : dayOrders) {
        List<OrderItem> lines = orderItemsMap.get(order.getId());
        BigDecimal total = lines.stream()
                .map(OrderItem::getPrice)
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
        result.add(new OrderSummary(order.getId(), lines.size(), total));
    }
    return result;
}
```

Add a finally block to ensure the connection is closed properly, or use try-with-resources to manage the connection and statement automatically. For example:

```java
public long countActive() throws SQLException {
    try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection();
         PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE active = true")) {
        ResultSet rs = statement.executeQuery();
        rs.next();
        return rs.getLong(1);
    }
}
```

